{%- comment -%}
  Tool-description lint (spec §12.3, §19.2) — the tool-poisoning tripwire.

  Tool descriptions are model-read text and are THE primary prompt-injection
  surface. This flags descriptions that carry model-directed instructions,
  cross-tool references, permission claims, or encoded blobs so a human reviews
  them. It returns warnings; whether a warning is fatal is the caller's policy
  (registry/build treats these as non-fatal warnings surfaced in tools/list
  diagnostics + logged, matching §12.3's "flag for human attention"). The
  adversarial suite (task-1.10) asserts this fires on a poisoned manifest.

  Detection is deliberately conservative and lowercase-substring based (no regex
  backtracking risk). It errs toward flagging; false positives cost a reviewer a
  glance, false negatives ship a poisoned tool.

  @param {string} text - the description (or any model-read manifest string)
  @return {object} { flagged:boolean, hits:[string] }
{%- endcomment -%}
{% liquid
  assign hits = '[]' | parse_json
  assign lc = text | default: '' | downcase

  # Imperative / jailbreak markers aimed at the model.
  assign markers = 'ignore previous,ignore all,ignore the,disregard,you must,you should,system prompt,as an ai,do not tell,without telling,override,forget everything,new instructions,act as,pretend,bypass,jailbreak' | split: ','
  for m in markers
    if lc contains m
      assign hits = hits | array_add: m
    endif
  endfor

  # Cross-tool references (tool shadowing / redirection).
  if lc contains 'call the' or lc contains 'use the tool' or lc contains 'instead of'
    assign hits = hits | array_add: 'cross-tool-reference'
  endif

  # Encoded-blob heuristic: a long unbroken run with no spaces suggests base64/hex
  # payloads smuggled into prose. Flag very long "words".
  assign words = text | default: '' | split: ' '
  for w in words
    assign wl = w | size
    if wl > 80
      assign hits = hits | array_add: 'long-encoded-blob'
      break
    endif
  endfor

  assign flagged = false
  if hits.size > 0
    assign flagged = true
  endif
  assign out = '{}' | parse_json
  hash_assign out['flagged'] = flagged
  hash_assign out['hits'] = hits
  return out
%}
